  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 359 PM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nAfternoon RAP analysis depicts a slow moving surface low over \nIllinois and Indiana, with high pressure beginning to build into \nUpper Michigan from northern Ontario. Northerly low level flow \nbetween these features has kept much of the Lake Superior shoreline \nshrouded in low stratus and fog through much of the day, although \nwebcam imagery from around the area generally shows fog dissipating \nand visibility improving as of mid afternoon. Starting to see a few \nshowers pop up on radar along the northern shores of lake Michigan \nand the WI border near a remnant frontal boundary analyzed through \nthe Mackinac Straits and the southcentral UP. Could continue to see \na few rumbles of thunder with this activity into the evening hours \ngiven modest instability on the order of 1000+ J/kg MUCAPE per \nlatest SPC mesoanalysis, but would not expect much storm \norganization given meager wind shear. Any lingering shower activity \nwill wind down tonight, giving way to drier northerly flow as the \nlow and associated upper level trough continue to depart to the \neast. This will yield clearing skies and likely less fog coverage \ntonight as drier air works into the low levels of the atmosphere, \nbut still could see some patchy fog in a few spots that have seen \nrecent rainfall.\n\nExpect pleasant sunny conditions on Sunday in dry northerly flow, \nwith highs again holding in the 70s with cooler temps near Lake \nSuperior. Clear skies and low dewpoints will make for a chilly \nSunday night as temps dip as low as the low 40s in some interior \nlocations, but do not expect temps to get quite low enough to \nwarrant any frost/freeze consideration. Dry weather persists into \nMonday under high pressure, with temperatures warming back into the \nupper 70s to lower 80s as 850 mb temps warm to 15-17 C. The next \nchance of precipitation then comes on Tuesday as a trough approaches \nfrom the northwest and drives another cold front through the UP. \nThis could also bring another round of thunderstorms to the area, \nbut severe potential is TBD given uncertainty over timing and degree \nof instability and shear. Temperatures look to drop down into the \n70s again on Wednesday behind the front, but will quickly recover \nback into the 80s on Thursday as additional systems bring more \nchances for precipitation towards the end of the week.",
